      "impact": "The central diagrams still require sustained implementation-level reading before readers can locate the relevant expansion. The reported reduction percentages do not translate consistently into a strong overview/detail hierarchy.",
      "recommendation": "Regenerate these central trees around the relationships needed to orient readers: major owners, inputs, transitions and publication boundaries. Move constructor inventories, numeric limits and detailed cleanup sequences into the relevant existing peripherals, retaining only details essential to understanding the central relationship. Let each point's subject determine its structure and depth."
    },
    {
      "evidence": "source/central L35–46 already explains definition freshness checks and session ownership, substantially repeated in source/lsp L2–15 and L22–28. editor/central L18–21 repeats session lifecycle covered by editor/session L2–6, and editor/central L70–72 repeats dirty-state rules in editor/session L19–22. generation/central L55–64 repeats retention and runtime lifetime developed in generation/lifetime L2–18.",
      "impact": "Several expansions ask readers to reread the overview before reaching new information. This increases the collection's 2,645-line reading burden and weakens the distinction between summary and drill-down.",
      "recommendation": "Give each repeated concept a primary explanatory location. Keep a short consequence or contract in the central tree, then make the peripheral begin with the additional decisions, exceptions or mechanisms. Preserve repetition only when needed to make an independently readable peripheral understandable."
    },
